WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.425
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:08.740
SPEAKER 1: Cloud Spanner is
a no-compromise database,

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:11.950
combining the abilities
of a relational database

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:14.170
with horizontal scale.

00:00:14.170 --> 00:00:18.440
It allows you to launch faster
and enjoy less administration.

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:21.430
This Quick Start shows you how
to perform basic operations

00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:25.780
in Cloud Spanner using the
Google Cloud Platform Console.

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:27.610
When you first
use Cloud Spanner,

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:29.500
you must create
an instance, which

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:32.860
is an allocation of resources
that are used by Cloud Spanner

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:35.680
databases in that instance.

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:37.840
Click Create Instance.

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:42.070
We used Test Instance as the
name and ID of this instance.

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:45.340
Choose Regional Configuration,
and pick any configuration

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:47.290
from the dropdown menu.

00:00:47.290 --> 00:00:49.030
Your instance
configuration determines

00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:51.640
the geographic location
where your instances

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:53.030
are stored and replicated.

00:00:53.030 --> 00:00:56.650
We'll keep the one for the
node count, and click Create.

00:00:56.650 --> 00:00:59.890
Your instance appears in
the Spanner Instances page.

00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:03.130
Now it's time to
create a database.

00:01:03.130 --> 00:01:05.950
Go ahead and click
Create Database.

00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:09.340
For the database name,
enter Example DB.

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:12.900
We'll skip the Define Your
Database Schema step for now.

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:14.590
We'll do that in
the next section.

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:15.940
Click Create.

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:18.820
Cloud Platform Console
displays some database details

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:21.460
after the database
has been created.

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:23.680
Next up, we'll
create a table schema

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:27.160
for your database using the
Cloud Spanner Data Definition

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:29.380
Language, or DDL.

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:30.760
Click Create Table.

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:34.840
And we'll flip the toggle
over to Edit as Text.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:39.040
And in the DDL statement,
enter the following statement.

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:40.690
Click Create Table.

00:01:40.690 --> 00:01:43.030
This may take a few
minutes to finish.

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:46.420
When the operation is complete,
the Cloud Platform Console

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:50.110
displays the details of the
table that you just created.

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:52.750
The Cloud Platform Console
provides an interface

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:56.410
for inserting, editing,
and deleting data.

00:01:56.410 --> 00:01:58.190
We'll talk about
each one in turn.

00:01:58.190 --> 00:01:59.770
Let's start with inserting.

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:04.300
Click Data and click Insert,
and type in the following data.

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:08.410
We'll do just the first
name and last name for now.

00:02:08.410 --> 00:02:10.600
And then click Save.

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:15.120
Notice now, the Singers
table has one row.

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:18.316
Let's click Insert again
and add an additional row.

00:02:18.316 --> 00:02:22.160
Enter the following
values for our second row.

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:30.200
And click Save.

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:32.540
Notice the Singers
table now has two rows.

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:35.810
You can also insert empty string
values when entering data.

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:38.090
We'll insert a third row.

00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:42.080
This time, in addition to
the integer ID key of 3,

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:48.140
we'll just enter a first name
and leave the last name blank.

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:49.420
Click Save.

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:52.330
And notice now that the
Singers table has three rows.

00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:56.560
And the row for singer
ID 3 has a last name

00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:58.970
that is an empty string.

00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:01.305
Let's take a look
at how to edit data.

00:03:01.305 --> 00:03:05.170
We'll add a birth date to
that third row of data.

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:07.990
Check the box, and click Edit.

00:03:07.990 --> 00:03:15.220
Enter the following values for
Birth Date, and click Save.

00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:18.010
We see now that the
row for singer ID 3

00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:21.620
in the Singers table
has a birth date value.

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:24.040
Let's talk about
how to delete data.

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:26.680
Deleting data is very
similar to editing.

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:30.310
Go ahead and check the box next
to the row for singer ID 2,

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:32.170
and click Delete.

00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:33.790
In the dialog that
appears, click

00:03:33.790 --> 00:03:36.100
OK to confirm the deletion.

00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:39.820
Notice now that the Singers
table has just two rows.

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:42.610
Next, let's run a
SQL statement query.

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:45.040
From the Tables
page, click Query.

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:48.880
Now, just click Run Query to
run the prepopulated query.

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:50.560
The Cloud Platform
Console displays

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:52.300
the results of your query.

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:53.590
Congratulations.

00:03:53.590 --> 00:03:56.800
You've successfully created
a Cloud Spanner database

00:03:56.800 --> 00:04:00.850
and executed a SQL statement
using the query editor.

00:04:00.850 --> 00:04:03.600
[MUSIC PLAYING]

